e william  here prince william is wearing an omega  seamaster 300 professional  this is a model that was popularized by  pierce brosnan's portrayal of james bond  in goldeneye  in 1996. it sort of signaled a  change in omega's design language and  one that they're still using today  i assume that prince williams does not  do any of the pretty cool things that  pierce brosnan's dead it's got no lasers  or bomb  detonators i imagine but it's still a  great look and it's a great classic  design  [Music]  matt scannell singer songwriter and  frontman of the great band vertical  horizon  matt has a wonderful collection of  vintage watches his love  is definitely focused on the omega  speedmaster the omega speedmaster was  chosen by nasa to accompany man on our  greatest accomplishment  landing on the moon this particular  watch which dates to the 1970s has a  tremendously beautiful  patina the tritium indices and hands  have faded to this creamy color and most  interestingly it has a double signature  on the dial  from the retailer meister the study of  omega speedmasters is a dense one  it is one of the most important and  popular vintage watches  of all time it is iconic in that it has  barely changed since its introduction in  1957. in fact yes  you can go out to an omega authorized  dealer right now and buy a watch that  looks  a whole lot like the ones that they were  producing in the 50s and 60s  the history is really really vibrant  it's interesting because the speedmaster  wasn't actually originally designed  to go to space it was actually designed  for  racing applications hence the name  speedmaster the  development of this watch has been  lockstep with advancements in  timekeeping and they're actually going  back to some of their routes  introducing new versions of some of  their earlier movements which  in an interesting twist of fate are  preferred by  many collectors  [Music]  in an earlier video we looked at kanye  west's cartier crash  here we're looking at angelina jolie  wearing an absolutely beautiful  vintage tank louis cartier this is the  watch that allegedly  proved the inspiration for the crash  when it came back  deformed from the heat of a car accident  the cartier tank louie drew its original  design inspiration from  world war one french battle tanks and as  you can see in the profile it features  two elongated edges  with a very simple and luxurious dial  printed roman indices  and blued steel hands these would have  featured an early  manual winding movement and were just as  beautiful then as they  are now and vice versa absolutely  timeless design  carl lagerfeld here we see the late  fashion tycoon  wearing an audemars piguet royal oak  this model is referred to as the jumbo  it was introduced in 1972 and was  designed by gerald genta for audemars  piguet  this watch was a absolute hail mary  during absolute  worst time in the swiss watchmaking  industry a time we refer to  as the quartz crisis this watch cost  way more than a gold rolex despite the  fact that it was produced in steel  it was a runaway success and is credited  with saving  the luxury sport watch category of the  industry  in fact it may have even created that  entire category all  its own carl lagerfeld is wearing this  watch  and he's got a couple interesting things  going on here one is that it's black  it might have been the first example of  a customized black watch  uh in history and secondly he's wearing  it over the cuff there's not too many  guys i know who could pull that off but  when you're karl lagerfeld  you want to paint your watch black and  wear it over your cuff  who's going to stop you collecting  vintage watches is one of the most  rewarding  components of wristwatch collecting  unlike modern watches these things each  have their own story and design  they have their own heritage they have  their own patina they're all unique  they're all  beautiful snowflakes but it's also a bit  of a minefield  you want to make sure that you follow a  few simple steps to make sure that  you're not getting taken  first and foremost buy the seller make  sure you're buying a watch from somebody  you know and can trust  secondly condition condition and  condition don't let anyone tell you that  you should be only looking at mint  condition watches in fact  patina and wear are part of the story  and part of the charm of these great old  things  but you do want to make sure that you're  buying what you want and you're not  being talked into  or settling for something less than you  want lastly  probably pay a little bit more than you  think you should because if it's a great  watch  and it's market value there's probably  something wrong with it  somebody would own it already so maybe  pay tomorrow's price or next month's  price  but if you follow those earlier steps  buying the seller and buying a great  watch  from the start you'll never have  anything to regret
 

